PgBouncer SCRAM-SHA-256 AUTH FLOW (the 4 auth packets)
=======================================================
 
SETUP USED
----------
Start PgBouncer with the TLS key logger preloaded:
 
  sudo -u pgbouncer env LD_PRELOAD=/home/hpuser/sslkeylog.so \
       SSLKEYLOGFILE=/var/tmp/keys/pgbouncer \
       pgbouncer -q /etc/pgbouncer/pgbouncer.ini
 
Connect with TLS disabled on the client side:
 
  PGSSLMODE=disable psql -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6432 -U postgres -d postgres
 
Because the client leg uses sslmode=disable, the traffic between psql and
PgBouncer (port 6432) is plain text. The 4 auth packets can be read directly in
Wireshark with the "pgsql" display filter and no key file. The SSLKEYLOGFILE
is only needed to decrypt the PgBouncer -> PostgreSQL leg if that leg uses TLS.
 
(Note: the config file is pgbouncer.ini. Your command had pgbouncer.cgf.)
 
 
THE PLAYERS
-----------
  psql        the client. Knows the password.
  PgBouncer   verifies the client, then logs in to PostgreSQL on its behalf.
  PostgreSQL  holds the SCRAM secret for the role in pg_authid / pg_shadow.
 
The stored SCRAM secret looks like this:
 
  SCRAM-SHA-256$4096:<salt>$<StoredKey>:<ServerKey>
 
  4096        iteration count
  salt        random salt chosen when the password was set
  StoredKey   SHA256(ClientKey)
  ServerKey   HMAC(SaltedPassword, "Server Key")
 
The password itself is never stored, and neither is ClientKey.
PgBouncer gets this same secret from userlist.txt or from auth_query
(which reads pg_shadow).
 
 
KEY DEFINITIONS
---------------
  SaltedPassword  = PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256(password, salt, iterations)
  ClientKey       = HMAC(SaltedPassword, "Client Key")
  StoredKey       = SHA256(ClientKey)
  ServerKey       = HMAC(SaltedPassword, "Server Key")
 
  AuthMessage     = client-first-bare + "," + server-first + ","
                    + client-final-without-proof
 
  ClientSignature = HMAC(StoredKey, AuthMessage)
  ClientProof     = ClientKey XOR ClientSignature
  ServerSignature = HMAC(ServerKey, AuthMessage)
 
 
STARTUP
-------
psql  -->  PgBouncer : StartupMessage (user, database, application_name, ...)
PgBouncer -->  psql  : AuthenticationSASL
                       (type 'R', code 10, mechanism list: SCRAM-SHA-256)
 
 
PHASE 1: psql <-> PgBouncer (the 4 auth packets)
------------------------------------------------
 
PACKET 1  psql -> PgBouncer   SASLInitialResponse  (client-first-message)
  Content:   n,,n=,r=<client-nonce>
  Meaning:   psql picks SCRAM-SHA-256 and sends a random nonce.
             The username field is empty because the user is already known
             from the StartupMessage.
 
PACKET 2  PgBouncer -> psql   AuthenticationSASLContinue  (server-first-message)
  Content:   r=<client-nonce><server-nonce>,s=<salt>,i=4096
  Meaning:   PgBouncer looks up the user's secret and returns the salt and
             iteration count from it. It appends its own nonce.
             PgBouncer uses the salt and iteration count of the real
             PostgreSQL secret, which matters in Phase 2.
 
PACKET 3  psql -> PgBouncer   SASLResponse  (client-final-message)
  Content:   c=biws,r=<combined-nonce>,p=<ClientProof>
  Meaning:   psql derives SaltedPassword from the password, salt and
             iterations, computes ClientKey and StoredKey, then builds
             AuthMessage and ClientProof = ClientKey XOR ClientSignature.
             The password and ClientKey are never sent. Only the proof is.
 
PACKET 4  PgBouncer -> psql   AuthenticationSASLFinal  (server-final-message)
  Content:   v=<ServerSignature>
  Meaning:   PgBouncer proves it also knows the secret. psql checks the
             signature, so the client knows the server is genuine.
  Followed by: AuthenticationOk (type 'R', code 0)
 
 
HOW PGBOUNCER VERIFIES THE CLIENT (between packets 3 and 4)
-----------------------------------------------------------
PgBouncer has StoredKey and ServerKey from the user's secret, but not the
password and not ClientKey. When packet 3 arrives it does this:
 
  1. Builds AuthMessage from packets 1, 2 and 3 (without the proof).
 
  2. ClientSignature = HMAC(StoredKey, AuthMessage)
     It can compute this because it has StoredKey.
 
  3. Recovers the key from the proof:
       ClientKey = ClientProof XOR ClientSignature
 
  4. Checks:  SHA256(ClientKey) == StoredKey
       match     -> the client knew the password. Authentication succeeds.
       mismatch  -> wrong password. Connection rejected.
 
  5. Builds the reply for packet 4:
       ServerSignature = HMAC(ServerKey, AuthMessage)
     ServerKey is what lets PgBouncer prove itself to the client.
 
Result: StoredKey checks the client, ServerKey proves the server, and the
ClientKey that falls out of step 3 is the useful by-product.
 
 
PHASE 2: PgBouncer <-> PostgreSQL (SCRAM passthrough)
-----------------------------------------------------
PgBouncer now needs a server connection from its pool. PostgreSQL will also
ask for SCRAM, and PgBouncer does not know the password. It uses the
ClientKey it recovered in step 3 above.
 
PgBouncer repeats the same 4 packets as the client side of the exchange:
 
  PgBouncer -> PostgreSQL : client-first  (n,,n=,r=<pgbouncer-nonce>)
  PostgreSQL -> PgBouncer : server-first  (r=...,s=<salt>,i=4096)
  PgBouncer -> PostgreSQL : client-final  (c=biws,r=...,p=<ClientProof>)
  PostgreSQL -> PgBouncer : server-final  (v=<ServerSignature>)
 
To build its ClientProof, PgBouncer computes:
 
  ClientSignature = HMAC(StoredKey, AuthMessage')
  ClientProof     = ClientKey XOR ClientSignature
 
  ClientKey   the one recovered from the psql client in Phase 1
  StoredKey   from the stored secret (userlist.txt or auth_query)
  AuthMessage' built from this new exchange, with PostgreSQL's nonce and salt
 
PgBouncer then checks PostgreSQL's ServerSignature with the ServerKey from
the stored secret. If it matches, the server is genuine.
 
PostgreSQL checks the proof exactly the way PgBouncer did in Phase 1:
  ClientKey' = ClientProof XOR HMAC(StoredKey, AuthMessage')
  SHA256(ClientKey') == StoredKey  -> accepted.
 
This works because the secret in PgBouncer matches the one in PostgreSQL
(same salt, same iterations, same StoredKey and ServerKey). It is also why
PgBouncer can log in to the database without a plaintext password.
 
 
COMPLETING THE CONNECTION
-------------------------
  PostgreSQL -> PgBouncer : AuthenticationOk, ParameterStatus..., BackendKeyData,
                            ReadyForQuery
  PgBouncer  -> psql      : AuthenticationOk, ParameterStatus..., BackendKeyData,
                            ReadyForQuery
 
From here, queries from psql are forwarded over the pooled server connection.
When the session or transaction ends (depending on pool_mode), the server
connection goes back to the pool and is reused.
 
 
LIMITS TO REMEMBER
------------------
  - The passthrough only works when PgBouncer has a SCRAM secret for the
    user that matches PostgreSQL's. If PgBouncer instead has a plaintext
    password or an md5 hash in userlist.txt, it cannot rebuild a SCRAM proof
    from a ClientKey and uses a different path.
  - The recovered ClientKey is kept in memory by PgBouncer for reuse on later
    server logins for that user. It is never written to the logs.
  - If the password changes in PostgreSQL, the salt or iteration count changes
    too, and PgBouncer's copy of the secret must be updated or server logins
    will fail.
 
 
WHAT YOU SEE IN WIRESHARK
-------------------------
Filter on port 6432 and decode as PostgreSQL. The order of messages is:
 
  StartupMessage
  Authentication request: SASL                 (R, code 10)
  SASLInitialResponse                          (p)   packet 1
  Authentication: SASL Continue                (R, code 11)  packet 2
  SASLResponse                                 (p)   packet 3
  Authentication: SASL Final                   (R, code 12)  packet 4
  Authentication request: OK                   (R, code 0)
  ParameterStatus ..., ReadyForQuery
 
The packets between PgBouncer and PostgreSQL on port 5432 show the same
sequence again for Phase 2.
 
